<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Third Dynasty of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2686–2613 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Third Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> ruled from about 2686 to 2613 BCE and opened the Old Kingdom. Its best-known king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a>, was buried in the Step Pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>, designed by his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a> and the first monumental stone building in Egypt. The dynasty strengthened central government from Memphis and was followed by the Fourth Dynasty, the age of the great pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Third Dynasty of Egypt" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Argenberg · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saqqara,_Pyramid_of_Djoser,_Ancient_Egypt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Third Dynasty was the first dynasty of the Old Kingdom, whose capital was at Memphis. It followed the turbulent last years of the Second Dynasty, which may have included civil war. Egyptologists now widely believe that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a> founded it as the direct successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khasekhemwy">Khasekhemwy</a>, the last king of the Second Dynasty. Seals naming Djoser were found at the entrance to Khasekhemwy's tomb at Abydos, which shows that Djoser buried him and finished the tomb. Earlier writers such as Wilkinson in 1999 proposed Nebka, also called Sanakht, as founder. Nebka, Sanakht and Sekhemkhet may have been brothers, sons of Khasekhemwy and his favoured consort Nimaathap.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The sources do not agree on the kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a> and the Abydos King List each record five, the Saqqara Tablet four, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> nine, many of whom did not exist or are the same king under several names. The Turin King List gives Nebka, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a>, Djoserti, Hudjefa I and Huni. The archaeological evidence points to the order Djoser, Sekhemkhet, with the Nebty name Djeserty, and Huni, the last king, who may be the same person as Qahedjet or less likely Khaba. The dynasty is credited with about 75 years, but dating is hard. Shaw gives approximately 2686 to 2613 BCE, Baines and Malek 2650 to 2575 BCE, and Dodson and Hilton 2584 to 2520 BCE.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a> is the best-known king, for commissioning his vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a> to build the Step Pyramid, the earliest surviving pyramid. The scribe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin-king-list">Turin King List</a> wrote his name in red ink, a sign of his importance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>. Some authorities believe Imhotep lived into the reign of Huni. Sekhemkhet's reign is thought to have lasted only six or seven years, and his tomb, the Buried Pyramid, was left unfinished. Khaba possibly built the Layer Pyramid at Zawyet el'Aryan, much smaller than intended; whether erosion or an unfinished building explains this is not known.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Reign lengths for Nebka, eighteen years in both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> and the Turin Canon, rest on sources written over 1,400 and 2,300 years after his lifetime, so their accuracy is uncertain. Little archaeological evidence survives for Nebka or Sanakht. Some scholars have proposed a southern origin for the dynasty: Flinders Petrie saw it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> from iconographic evidence, while S.O.Y. Keita thought southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> equally likely.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Third Dynasty of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors of the <b>Third Dynasty</b>, from around 2686 BCE, opened the Old Kingdom of Egypt and its age of pyramid building.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-dynastic-egypt">Early Dynastic Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
